From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 4 Sep 2026 06:50:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] winex11.drv: glFinish before reading back an offscreen swap on
 *both* present paths.

Patch 0010 added a glFinish() to x11drv_surface_swap for the case where
GLX_OML_sync_control is missing, and guarded it with `!pglXWaitForSbcOML` on
the reasoning that where the extension exists, glXWaitForSbcOML already waits.

That guard is wrong, and it fails in the worst possible direction: it skips
the fix on exactly the machines that have working hardware GL.

Reported on a real desktop (:0, Xwayland/GNOME, AMD RX 6900 XT, which does
advertise GLX_OML_sync_control): Word's licensing dialog paints, is briefly
legible, and then goes black -- and moving the pointer over a control makes
that part of the text reappear, which is a repaint transiently winning a race,
not a drawing failure. Measured on the dialog's own X window (375x178):

    before   176 distinct colours, 51772/66750 pixels black (77%)
    after    545 distinct colours,     0/66750 pixels black

The reason the OML path needs it too: glXWaitForSbcOML waits for the swap to
be *processed*, which under Xwayland with a compositor means handed over to
the compositor -- not "the pixels are in the window and can be read back".
client_surface_present reads this drawable from a different thread the moment
this function returns, so what it needs is a guarantee about readability, and
glFinish is the only call here that gives one.

So the condition becomes simply `offscreen`. The comment now records both
failure modes, because they have different causes and the same symptom, and
the previous comment's reasoning is exactly what kept the fix from running
where it was needed.

**This is not the whole fault, and the patch should not be read as closing
it.** With it applied the same user reports the inverse symptom on the same
desktop: the dialog is now correct at rest and *goes black under the pointer*,
where before it was black at rest and legible under the pointer. Measured
after the change, on the dialog's own window and on the four shadow windows,
with the pointer parked on a button and away from it:

    dialog window   0% black, 545 colours   -- identical in both positions
    shadow strips   alpha [1,1,3,5] / [0,0,0,0], rgb 0 -- identical in both

So every surface this tree owns holds correct pixels in both states, and what
changes is downstream of them. That last step could not be measured here:
`import -window root` is refused under Xwayland, so the composited result --
the only thing that actually differs -- is not readable from this side. The
honest summary is that this patch fixes a real and separately-verified
readback race (77% black -> 0% black on the window's own pixels) and that a
second fault, on the compositor side of the boundary, remains and is visible
only to a person looking at the screen.
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/opengl.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/opengl.c
@@ -1478,7 +1478,37 @@
         if (pglXWaitForSbcOML) pglXWaitForSbcOML( gdi_display, gl->drawable, target_sbc, &ust, &msc, &sbc );
     }
 
-    if (offscreen && !pglXWaitForSbcOML) XFlush( gdi_display );
+    if (offscreen)
+    {
+        /* altars: client_surface_present below reads this drawable's pixels
+         * from a different thread the moment this returns, so the swap has to
+         * be genuinely complete *and readable* first. Neither path above
+         * guarantees that:
+         *
+         *   - Without GLX_OML_sync_control (a software or virtual GL
+         *     renderer) nothing waited at all; XFlush only flushes the X11
+         *     protocol stream, not the renderer. Measured as a real race
+         *     (patches/office/0009 and "the missing measurement, taken" in
+         *     docs/office365-under-wine.md): identical content drawn every
+         *     time, read back black on roughly half of presents.
+         *
+         *   - *With* it -- true on a hardware GL desktop -- glXWaitForSbcOML
+         *     waits for the swap to be processed, which under Xwayland plus a
+         *     compositor means handed to the compositor, not "the pixels are
+         *     now in the window and can be read back". The condition this
+         *     replaces (`!pglXWaitForSbcOML`) therefore skipped the fix on
+         *     exactly the machines that have working GL, and the same fault
+         *     was reported there: Word's licensing dialog paints, is briefly
+         *     legible, then reads back mostly black (measured on :0 with an
+         *     AMD GPU -- the dialog's own window, 375x178, 77% black pixels
+         *     while its buttons still render).
+         *
+         * glFinish() is the wait that actually holds in both cases. XFlush
+         * stays for the X-side request, the same order the sibling .flush
+         * callbacks below already use for the same reason. */
+        funcs->p_glFinish();
+        XFlush( gdi_display );
+    }
 
     client_surface_present( base->client );
     return TRUE;
